### When do you throw IllegalArgumentException?

Throw IAE when the method's arguments are invalid. The caller can fix this by passing valid input.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Objects;

public class IAEDemo {

    public static void withdraw(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        if (accountId == null || accountId.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("accountId must not be blank");
        }
        if (amount == null || amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("amount must be positive: " + amount);
        }
        System.out.println("Withdrawn Rs." + amount + " from " + accountId);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        withdraw("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("5000"));
        try {
            withdraw("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("-100"));
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("IAE: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Withdrawn Rs.5000 from SAV2001
IAE: amount must be positive: -100
```

### When do you throw IllegalStateException?

Throw ISE when the object is in the wrong state for the requested operation.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ISEDemo {

    static class TradingSession {
        private boolean open = false;

        void open() { open = true; System.out.println("Session opened"); }
        void close() { open = false; System.out.println("Session closed"); }

        void executeTrade(String tradeId) {
            if (!open) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("Trading session is closed. Call open() first.");
            }
            System.out.println("Executed: " + tradeId);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TradingSession session = new TradingSession();
        try {
            session.executeTrade("TRD-001");  // ISE: session not open
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("ISE: " + e.getMessage());
        }
        session.open();
        session.executeTrade("TRD-001");  // works after open()
    }
}
```

```text
ISE: Trading session is closed. Call open() first.
Session opened
Executed: TRD-001
```

### How does Objects.requireNonNull work?

It is the JDK standard for null argument validation. It throws NullPointerException, not IAE, following the convention that null where non-null is required is signaled by NPE.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Objects;

public class RequireNonNullDemo {

    public static void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(from, "from account must not be null");
        Objects.requireNonNull(to, "to account must not be null");
        Objects.requireNonNull(amount, "amount must not be null");
        System.out.println("Transfer Rs." + amount + " from " + from + " to " + to);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        transfer("SAV2001", "CUR3001", new BigDecimal("10000"));
        try {
            transfer(null, "CUR3001", new BigDecimal("10000"));
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            System.out.println("NPE: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Transfer Rs.10000 from SAV2001 to CUR3001
NPE: from account must not be null
```

### What is the decision guide?

Ask: would this method work if the caller passed DIFFERENT arguments?

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class DecisionGuideDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Decision guide:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Q: Would different arguments fix the problem?");
        System.out.println("  YES → IllegalStateException (state is wrong)");
        System.out.println("  NO  → IllegalArgumentException (input is wrong)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Q: Is the problem WHAT was passed or WHEN it was called?");
        System.out.println("  WHAT → IAE");
        System.out.println("  WHEN → ISE");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("HTTP mapping:");
        System.out.println("  IAE → 400 Bad Request (client fix: send valid data)");
        System.out.println("  ISE → 409 Conflict or 503 (server state problem)");
    }
}
```

```text
Decision guide:

Q: Would different arguments fix the problem?
  YES → IllegalStateException (state is wrong)
  NO  → IllegalArgumentException (input is wrong)

Q: Is the problem WHAT was passed or WHEN it was called?
  WHAT → IAE
  WHEN → ISE

HTTP mapping:
  IAE → 400 Bad Request (client fix: send valid data)
  ISE → 409 Conflict or 503 (server state problem)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **NPE vs IAE for null.** Joshua Bloch (Effective Java) recommends NPE for null arguments. Objects.requireNonNull follows this convention. Some teams prefer IAE for null. Be consistent.
- **Guava Preconditions.** `checkArgument(condition, "msg")` throws IAE. `checkState(condition, "msg")` throws ISE. More concise than manual if-throw.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does Objects.requireNonNull throw NPE or IAE? (NPE by design.)
- When is ISE appropriate? (Object in wrong state: closed, consumed, already initialized.)
- What HTTP status maps to IAE vs ISE? (IAE = 400, ISE = 409 or 503.)
